**Node.js (built-in `crypto`, no new dependency):**
```js
const { scrypt, randomBytes } = require('crypto');
const { promisify } = require('util');
const scryptAsync = promisify(scrypt);

const salt = randomBytes(16);
const keyLen = 64;
const params = { N: 2 ** 14, r: 8, p: 1 };          // ~16 MB memory, good starting cost
const hash = await scryptAsync(password, salt, keyLen, params);
```

Store it as one string so you can upgrade cost later (per our earlier decision):
```js
const stored = `scrypt$${params.N}$${params.r}$${params.p}$${salt.toString('base64')}$${hash.toString('base64')}`;
```

**Python equivalent:**
```python
import hashlib, os, base64
salt = os.urandom(16)
hash_ = hashlib.scrypt(password.encode(), salt=salt, n=2**14, r=8, p=1, dklen=64)
```

Verify with the library's constant-time compare (never `==` on hashes), and parse `N/r/p` out of the stored string at login so old hashes can be rehashed with newer params. If you'd rather go with Argon2id instead, say so now — but pick one today and write it in the README so the team doesn't drift.

